 "readme": "Clinical datasets are notoriously imbalanced: some rare conditions appear only a handful of times (or not at all), \nwhich makes it almost impossible to train downstream models or share open data.  \nLLM-CTGAN tackles that problem by modifying a Conditional Tabular GAN ([CTGAN](https://github.com/sdv-dev/CTGAN)) \nwith embeddings obtained from LLM summaries of each data point. \nThose embeddings capture the global clinical context (lab values, vitals) and are injected into the GAN as an additional conditional vector. \nBy conditioning on the corresponding LLM embedding,\nthe generator learns the latent neighbourhood of a rare disease even if the disease itself is undersampled. \n\n![train](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9dfa94f0-b8e3-4418-abdf-9cac7041d422)\n\nAt sampling time we simply generate new embeddings of the disease that we want to generate and feed it to the generator. \n\n![inference](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/45c8439c-fca6-4a35-9bf2-69b50e25264d)\n\n## LLM-CTGAN \n(Folder llm_ctgan)\nlm_ctgan.py adds a second conditional pathway. The usual one-hot vector for each discrete column is concatenated with a fixed-length\nLLM embedding that is supplied per row. The combined vector goes through the same conditional batch-norm blocks as in vanilla CTGAN, so no other changes to the generator/discriminator \ncode are needed. During training the code samples exactly as CTGAN does, but it also adds the matching llm embeddings for each row. \n\nConditional sampling – During inference you can:\n- Un-conditioned: call model.sample(n) – the embeddings are drawn at random from the training set. \n- Conditioned on a label: model.sample(n, condition_column=\"icd_code\", condition_value=\"I820\") – the helper chooses a real row whose ICD = I820, grabs its embedding, \nand feeds both to the generator.\n- Conditioned on an embedding: if the llm embedding never existed in the training data, pass an embedding manually:\n\n```\nmodel.sample(n, condition_column=\"icd_code\",\n                condition_value=\"I820\",\n                embedding=my_budd_chiari_vector)\n```\n\n## LLM-CTGAN WITH HVL LOSS \n(Folder llm_cgan)\nllm_cgan.py is a modified version of LLM-CTGAN with vicinal loss. \n\n## Data\nThe structured dataset is taken from MIMIC-IV produced by the EDA script in datasets/mimic/EDA.ipynb.\n\n- Liver or heart related ICD-10 codes and corresponding subject_id/hadm_id pairs\t\n- 3\tdemographics and 11 vital sign columns; BP split into systolic/diastolic\n- 30 lab tests \n\nAfter pivoting wide and dropping columns with > 50 % missing values, we keep 24 numeric features + 2 categoricals (icd_code, gender).\nClass distribution is highly imbalanced (e.g. C787 = 2 141 rows, I820 = 44 rows).\n\n## Experiments\n\nnotebooks/MIMIC-example2/LLMCTGAN.ipynb shows the full workflow:\n\nSplit the cleaned table 80 / 20, stratified by ICD.\nFit both vanilla CTGAN and LLM-CTGAN on the training split (only one Budd-Chiari row is kept here).\nGenerate Budd-Chiari synthetic rows with each model, oversampling until the positive class ≈ 50 %.\nTrain three downstream classifiers (log-reg, random-forest, GBT) on real vs. synthetic data and evaluate on the untouched test set.\nMeasure fidelity with correlation heat-maps and KS-Complement. \n\n## Summaries \n\nThe summaries are done based on the data from the mimic dataset. For this different open source LLMs are used. The inference was performed in the Snellius cluster and the code for this \ncan be found in the Snellius folder. \n",
